      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Consider the following congruence.\n\n$$\n6≡\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\n$$\n\nWhich of the following values gives a true statement when inserted into the blank space?\n\n- $0$\n- $1$\n- $2$",
      "worked_solution": "To visualize congruence modulo $2,$ let's write the numbers $0,\\,1,\\,2,\\,3,…$ in a spiral around a circle so that each \"layer\" contains exactly $2$ numbers. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14114803/assets/93c588ff95bd8987e60ce2221ebef6e44c05039c13608ba35968a28c8582dd34.png)\n\nIn the diagram, each \"spoke\" of our wheel contains numbers that are congruent modulo $2.$ \n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each case in turn.\n\n- Case I gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-0).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-0)÷2=6÷2=3\n$$\n\n- Case II gives a false statement because $2∤(6-1).$ Notice that $6-1=5,$ which is not divisible by $2.$\n\n- Case III gives a true statement. Indeed, $2∣(6-2).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(6-2)÷2=4÷2=2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$0$ and $2$ only.\"",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
